 111   `test_seal.py:309-329`; lane-level rc/state mapping is not covered.
 112 - Inline mentions and marker lines with extra spaces do not count, as intended.
 113 
 114 ## Snapshot, path validation, and launch custody
 115 
 116 ### S1 — BLOCKER: charged-path exclusion is bypassable before the sandbox
 117 
 118 **Lines:** `lane.sh:228-287`; `test_lane_fallacy.py:399-440`.
 119 
 120 The check is lexical, case-sensitive, and applies `-L` only to the final
 121 component. All of these pass validation and are then opened by hash/copy before
 122 Seatbelt starts:
 123 
 124 - `charged_input=./jc2-lean/secret.md` (the exclusion pattern does not match);
 125 - `charged_input=JC2-LEAN/secret.md` on case-insensitive APFS;
 126 - `charged_input=refs/alias/secret.md` where `alias` is a symlink to the
 127   excluded tree or anywhere outside the repository.
 128 
 129 There is also a check/open race: a regular leaf can be swapped to a symlink
 130 after lines 263-267 and retained through the separate hash and `cp` opens at
 131 274-275. The copied bytes can therefore come from an excluded or external
 132 inode. The existing tests cover only literal lowercase `jc2-lean/...` and a
 133 literal `..` component. They would catch none of these scenarios.
 134 
 135 The purported ASCII check is locale-dependent shell pattern matching. Under
 136 `en_US.UTF-8` on this host, characters including `é`, `ø`, and `å` collate
 137 inside `[A-Za-z]` and pass lines 244-255. Absolute paths and consecutive `..`
 138 are rejected. Sequential ASCII case-only basename collisions are noticed by
 139 `-e` on default case-insensitive APFS, but uniqueness is not normalized by the
 140 code and therefore changes on case-sensitive APFS.
 141 
 142 **Minimal fix:** byte-validate under a forced `LC_ALL=C`; reject `.` as well as
 143 `..` components; case-fold the excluded name and basename uniqueness key; and
 144 walk from an opened repository dirfd with `openat`/`O_NOFOLLOW`, rejecting every
 145 symlink component. Hash and copy from that one opened regular-file descriptor.
 146 
 147 ### S2 — BLOCKER: the model can read mutable originals after they are frozen
 148 
 149 **Lines:** `lane.sh:263-310, 318-327, 500-518`.
 150 
 151 The profile is `allow default`. It protects the snapshot from writes, but does
 152 not deny reads of `$repo_root/$charged_rel`; the original declaration and path
 153 also remain in the delivered prompt. A source can change after snapshot, then
 154 the model can deliberately read the changed repository path rather than
 155 `{{LANE_INPUTS}}`. The receipt records `REPO_DRIFT` while the comment at
 156 500-501 assumes, without enforcement, that “the model read frozen bytes.” A
 157 hash proves which snapshot was offered, not which bytes were consumed.
 158 
 159 **Minimal fix:** run the provider with a read allowlist rooted in immutable
 160 custody and explicitly required runtime/configuration paths, or at least deny
 161 every charged original and alias. Publish only the snapshot locations in the
 162 model prompt. Add a test whose adapter successfully tries both the snapshot and
 163 the original after drift; the latter must be denied. The current drift test at
 164 `test_lane_fallacy.py:367-397` reads only the snapshot.
 165 
 166 ### S3 — BLOCKER: executed adapter and verification tools are mutable provenance
 167 
 168 **Lines:** `lane.sh:62-65, 87-99, 174-179, 375-380, 413-483`;
 169 `lane_detach.py:51-64, 225-229, 437-480`.
 170 
 171 Unlike the prompt, seal tool, and validator, the adapter is hashed at line 175
 172 but executed later from its live pathname. Replace it after hashing, execute
 173 the replacement, and restore it before line 477: the final receipt claims the
 174 original adapter digest. Detached launch adds a longer path-check/hash/launchd
 175 reopen window.
 176 
 177 Security-critical `python3`, SHA tools, and `sh` are also resolved repeatedly
 178 from inherited `PATH`, which detach deliberately forwards. A hostile adapter
 179 can write a fake `python3` or `sha256sum` into an earlier writable PATH
 180 directory before it exits. The parent can then accept a fake `status=CLEAN` or
 181 arbitrary digest text (the digest is not validated as 64 hex), producing a
 182 successful false receipt.
 183 
 184 **Minimal fix:** snapshot the adapter through one no-follow fd and execute that
 185 immutable copy; pin and attest absolute system interpreters/tools in a sanitized
 186 environment (prefer one trusted supervisor implementation); and validate every
 187 digest's syntax. Add adapter ABA and poisoned-PATH tests. No current test
 188 mutates the adapter or parent toolchain.
 189 
 190 ### S4 — MAJOR: report-path launch validation is only a substring test
 191 
 192 **Lines:** `lane.sh:214-219`; `test_lane_fallacy.py:357-365`.
 193 
 194 A prompt saying only “write `xmodel/tag.md.bak`”, quoting the path as a code
 195 example, or explicitly saying not to write it passes `grep -F`. The promised
 196 exact report-path contract is therefore not established before an expensive
 197 launch; the lane later fails only if no report happens to appear.
 198 
 199 **Minimal fix:** require one machine-readable declaration such as
 200 `report_path=xmodel/<tag>.md` and parse it exactly. Add `.md.bak`, prefix,
 201 negation, duplicate, CRLF, and code-example negatives; the existing test covers
 202 only total absence.
 203 
 204 ### S5 — MAJOR: contract violations do not consistently return rc 7
 205 
 206 **Lines:** `lane.sh:216-219, 239-280, 433-455`;
 207 `test_lane_fallacy.py:357-365, 399-440, 498-516`.
 208 
 209 Missing report-path and charged-input violations exit 2, and their tests
 210 explicitly require 2. Post-run missing/multiple-marker violations become 7
 211 only when the adapter had returned zero; an adapter rc 1 plus a markerless
 212 declared-contract report remains rc 1. This contradicts the stated typed rc 7
 213 contract and prevents reliable orchestration by failure class.
 214 
 215 **Minimal fix:** route every hardening-contract failure through one `exit 7`
 216 path and make boundary violations override/preserve the provider result in a
 217 separate receipt field. Update the tests to require 7, including a nonzero
 218 adapter plus malformed report.
 219 
 220 ### S6 — MAJOR: output creation follows dangling symlinks and mutable parents
 221 
 222 **Lines:** `lane.sh:84-107, 335-382`; `seal.py:52-78`.
 223 
 224 `mkdir -p` accepts symlinked `xmodel`/`.lane-locks`; `[ -e ]` misses dangling
 225 symlinks; and shell `>`/`>>` follows them. A dangling
 226 `xmodel/tag.run.v2 -> /chosen/path` passes duplicate detection and causes the
 227 receipt to be created outside `xmodel`; log creation has the same defect.
 228 `seal.py` resolves parent symlinks before checking them, so its apparent
 229 parent-symlink rejection sees only the canonical target. Retargeting the
 230 lexical parent can make emitted paths name different bytes.
 231 
 232 **Minimal fix:** reject symlinks in every directory component, open trusted
 233 directories once, and create all outputs with anchored
 234 `O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW`. Keep and verify their inode identities through final
 235 commit. Add dangling-final, symlink-parent, and parent-retarget tests; the seal
 236 symlink test at `test_seal.py:238-246` covers only the final component.
 237 
 238 ## Receipts and detach lifecycle
 239 
 240 ### R1 — BLOCKER: the provider can alter other lanes' permanent evidence
 241 
 242 **Lines:** `lane.sh:318-327, 375-380`;
 243 `test_lane_fallacy.py:264-278`.
 244 
 245 Seatbelt allows all writes except the excluded tree, custody temp, and the
 246 current run file. A provider in lane A may overwrite any prior report,
 247 `.run.v2`, raw, overflow, or log; delete/rename the `xmodel` parent; or pre-seed
 248 future-tag symlinks. Mode 400 is no defense against unlink/replacement by the
 249 same owner in a writable directory. The test probes only a direct overwrite of
 250 the current receipt and therefore gives false confidence about the evidence
 251 store as a whole.
 252 
 253 **Minimal fix:** the provider must write only inside a fresh per-run staging
 254 directory. Deny all provider writes to permanent `xmodel`, `.lane-jobs`, and
 255 lock state; after provider teardown, let the trusted parent validate and
 256 atomically publish report, log, sidecars, and receipt. Test writes to a prior
 257 and predicted-future tag plus parent-directory rename.
 258 
 259 ### R2 — BLOCKER: a background descendant makes `report_state` false
 260 
 261 **Lines:** `lane.sh:375-384, 399-457, 521-566`;
 262 `lane_detach.py:447-450`.
 263 
 264 The wrapper waits only for the direct `sandbox-exec` PID. An adapter can write a
 265 clean marked report, start a delayed background writer, and exit. While the
 266 wrapper seals and finalizes, that descendant still has permission to rewrite
 267 the report. It can act after `CLEAN` but before line 545, yielding one receipt
 268 with `report_state=BODY_SEALED`, `final_status=DONE`, and a hash of markerless
 269 bytes; acting after line 545 instead leaves a stale hash. A child that creates a
 270 new session/process group can survive launchd's same-process-group cleanup too.
 271 This also makes the final compare/replace race in D3 exploitable.
 272 
 273 **Minimal fix:** contain the provider in a dedicated killable job/process
 274 group, prevent daemonization or enumerate and reap every descendant, and prove
 275 the writer set empty before validation. Only then publish provider staging into
 276 a write-denied evidence directory. No current fake adapter backgrounds a
 277 writer, so the clean/divert receipt tests cannot catch this.
 278 
 279 ### R3 — BLOCKER: the lane can forge detached supervisor state
 280 
 281 **Lines:** `lane.sh:318-327`; `lane_detach.py:232-274, 293-305,
 282 595-630`.
 283 
 284 The sandbox permits writes to `.lane-jobs`. A still-running lane can create
 285 `.lane-jobs/<tag>/unloaded.v1.json`; `parse_launchd` trusts mere existence and
 286 returns `UNLOADED` without querying launchd. `unload` then skips `bootout` and
 287 prints success while the lane remains live. The lane can also unlink and
 288 replace `launch.v1.json`; `load_record` does not recompute the canonical label
 289 or domain or verify the recorded file hashes.
 290 
 291 **Minimal fix:** place supervisor state outside provider reach and anchor every
 292 open; validate label/domain and content digests. An unload marker must be a
 293 validated record installed only after confirmed successful `bootout`, and
 294 status must still confirm service absence. There are no detach tests; the
 295 current receipt-write test does not touch this plane.
 296 
 297 ### R4 — BLOCKER: a successful process can leave a missing/torn/fabricated receipt
 298 
 299 **Lines:** `lane.sh:283-284, 335-373, 382, 521-569`;
 300 `lane_detach.py:348-360, 525-549`.
 301 
 302 The input-manifest append, initial receipt block, child-PID append, and terminal
 303 receipt append are unchecked. They are direct writes to the authoritative
 304 pathname, with no fsynced atomic commit. ENOSPC or a write/permission failure
 305 can leave a receipt at `initial_status=RUNNING` (or no receipt), yet line 569
 306 still exits with the provider's zero and launchd reports `SUCCEEDED`.
 307 
 308 The line format is injectable too. Prompt paths are not CR/LF validated but are
 309 emitted raw at line 342; a legal filename containing
 310 `\nfinal_status=DONE` plants that key in the initial receipt. If the lane then
 311 dies before finalization, a consumer can see a fabricated terminal field;
 312 duplicate-key parsers can disagree after finalization.
 313 
 314 **Minimal fix:** use a structured, duplicate-rejecting encoding; reject control
 315 characters; build a complete terminal receipt in a staged file, fsync it and
 316 the directory, then atomically install it. Any publication/hash failure must
 317 force non-success. Add ENOSPC/short-write/kill and newline-filename tests.
 318 
 319 ### R5 — MAJOR: detached status treats brittle heuristics as process truth
 320 
 321 **Lines:** `lane_detach.py:281-363, 479-513, 525-558`.
 322 
 323 The parser regexes human-readable `launchctl print` output—explicitly not a
 324 stable API—and ignores terminating-signal/terminal-state variants. An inactive
 325 signal-killed job without the exact `last exit code = N` line becomes
 326 `STARTING` forever. If `launchctl print` fails, unauthenticated `pgrep -f`
 327 matching argv is promoted to `RUNNING` with a PID; an unrelated process can
 328 spoof it. Conversely, `command_launch` returns zero and prints the supervision
 329 reassurance even if its immediate parsed state is `MISSING_JOB`.
 330 
 331 **Minimal fix:** represent parse/visibility uncertainty as `UNKNOWN`, never as
 332 `RUNNING`, `STARTING`, or terminal success; bind a PID to uid, start time,
 333 executable, and launchd job identity; handle signal termination; and require a
 334 confirmed post-bootstrap state before returning success. Use mocked output for
 335 format variants, stale/dead PID, argv spoofing, and bootstrap timeout.
 336 
 337 ### R6 — MAJOR: cancellation and supervisor setup are not failure-atomic
 338 
 339 **Lines:** `lane.sh:122-155, 250-287, 386-397`;
 340 `lane_detach.py:172-181, 414-477, 561-575`.
 341 
 342 A TERM/HUP/INT during pre-launch preparation sets `caught_signal` while
 343 `child_pid` is empty, then the script can continue and launch the lane anyway;
 344 it labels the result cancelled only after the provider finishes. SIGKILL after
 345 lock creation leaves a stale lock and partial temp snapshot because traps
 346 cannot run. Detached `write_exclusive` similarly exposes final JSON names while
 347 writing: kill/ENOSPC leaves a corrupt tag directory that blocks retry, and one
 348 corrupt valid-tag record makes `iter_records` abort listing every other active
 349 lane.
 350 
 351 **Minimal fix:** make traps phase-aware and abort before spawn; use a bounded
 352 whole-group TERM/KILL protocol; give locks recoverable owner/start identity;
 353 and stage/fsync/rename supervisor records. Listing should emit a typed
 354 `CORRUPT` entry and continue. Existing cleanup tests exercise catchable normal
 355 exits only; there are no signal, kill-mid-write, or corrupt-list tests.
 356 
 357 ## Test-suite coverage
 358 
 359 The most consequential gaps are not redundant with existing tests:
 360 
 361 | Untested edge/regression | Would an existing test catch it? |
 362 |---|---|
 363 | Valid seal plus one provider-error byte remains `CLEAN_SEALED` | No. `test_seal.py:281-289` has no suffix and accepts a noncanonical fixture. |
 364 | EOF marker without newline becomes lane `BODY_SEALED` | No. `test_seal.py:87-97` catches verifier acceptance, not divert/lane acceptance. |
 365 | CRLF divert body/overflow byte boundary | No. `test_seal.py:134-147` covers verify only. |
 366 | Standalone marker inside a code fence; second marker at unterminated EOF | No. Inline and ordinary-double-marker tests do not exercise either. |
 367 | Non-UTF-8 overflow is banked byte-exactly | No. `test_seal.py:248-252` checks verifier rejection only. |
 368 | Occupied overflow leaves a raw artifact after failure | No. `test_seal.py:331-339` creates the defect but never asserts raw absence. |
 369 | Kill/ENOSPC/fsync failure during raw, overflow, report, receipt, or supervisor sidecar commit | No. Stamp-only mocks do not cover divert/receipt/detach transactions. |
 370 | `./jc2-lean`, uppercase APFS alias, ancestor symlink, leaf swap, absolute input, Unicode locale, or case-folded basename pair | No. `test_lane_fallacy.py:399-440` covers literal lowercase exclusion, missing file, and literal `..` only. |
 371 | Model reads drifted original instead of snapshot | No. `test_lane_fallacy.py:367-397` asks it to read only the snapshot. |
 372 | `.md.bak`/negated report-path mention | No. `test_lane_fallacy.py:357-365` tests total absence only. |
 373 | Provider writes prior/future custody files or renames their parent | No. `test_lane_fallacy.py:264-278` probes only the current run-file pathname. |
 374 | Background writer changes report after boundary classification | No adapter in the suite backgrounds or daemonizes. |
 375 | Adapter ABA replacement or provider-poisoned `PATH` | No executable/tool provenance test exists. |
 376 | Contract violation following provider rc nonzero | No. The marker test uses provider rc 0; launch-contract tests actually entrench rc 2. |
 377 | Same-tag concurrent detach, forged unload marker, corrupt sidecar, launchd format variants, dead/spoofed PID, bootstrap uncertainty | No charged test imports or invokes `lane_detach.py` at all. |
 378 
 379 One positive result: under cooperative state, atomic creation of
 380 `.lane-jobs/<tag>` followed by the lane lock makes an ordinary same-tag race
 381 unlikely to double-start the adapter. That protection is not sufficient in the
 382 actual threat model because the provider can mutate both supervisor/lock
 383 namespaces, and setup/kill recovery is not transactional.
 384 
 385 ## Verdict

